Worked example: gcd(48, 180). Euclid: 180 = 3×48 + 36; 48 = 1×36 + 12; 36 = 3×12 + 0. Last nonzero remainder: **12**. Python: math.gcd(48,180) = 12 — agreement. The extended algorithm also finds integers x,y with 48x+180y=gcd; those coefficients are exactly what the next note's inverse computation uses. GCD answers 'what's the largest shared structure' — from simplifying fractions to RSA key generation.

