a ≡ b (mod m) means m divides a−b. Worked examples mod 26: 17 + 10 = 27 ≡ 1; 17 × 3 = 51 ≡ 25; 100 mod 26 = **22** since 26×3=78 and 100−78=22. Addition, subtraction, multiplication all work mod m; division needs an inverse, which exists exactly when gcd(a,m)=1. Here gcd(17,26) = 1, so 17 has an inverse mod 26. Modern cryptography is number theory deployed: RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic curves.

