A truth table lists every assignment of the variables. With n variables there are 2^n rows — for n=3 that is **8** rows, computed as 2^3. Worked example: the implication `p -> q`, defined as `(NOT p) OR q`. Its table: p=T,q=T -> T; p=T,q=F -> F; p=F,q=T -> T; p=F,q=F -> T. The only false row is true-premise/false-conclusion. Enumerating all rows mechanically (as the lab does in Python) removes all doubt: no case is forgotten, because the count 2^n proves the table is complete.

