Bayes: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+). Worked example: 1% of requests are malicious (0.01); the detector catches 99% of malicious and flags 5% of benign. P(+) = 0.99×0.01 + 0.05×0.99 = **0.0594**. So P(malicious | flagged) = 0.0099/0.0594 = **0.167** — only about 1 in 6 flags is real. This is the base-rate effect: with rare events, even good tests produce mostly false positives. Every alert-fatigue argument in operations is Bayes in disguise.

