Before you touch an offensive tool, you build the lab and the habits. This is the exact sequence I’d run with a new operator, and it’s your Milestone 1.1. Everything here runs against systems you own.
1. **Build the lab first.** Two virtual machines on an isolated host-only network: an attacker Linux box and a target you own. No lab, no techniques — that’s the rule.
2. **Bash warm-up: pipeline a scan.** Run a scan of your lab target, then chain grep, cut, sort, and uniq to extract just the open ports and service banners. Text processing at speed is the skill; the scan is the excuse.
3. **Passive recon drill.** Against a domain you own: pull certificate-transparency names, DNS records, and WHOIS. Zero packets to the target’s ports — passive before active is ethics AND tradecraft.
4. **Scan with discipline.** One host deeply before ten hosts shallowly. Compare a connect scan against a SYN scan of the same lab host and note what each reveals — and what each would log.
5. **Identify, then crack, a lab hash.** Practice hash-identifier thinking on a hash you generated yourself, then attack it with a wordlist plus rules. Estimate the time first: keyspace divided by hashrate.
6. **Wi-Fi on your own access point.** Capture your own 4-way handshake, crack it offline, then enable WPA3 with PMF and confirm the attack no longer works. Every attack module ends in the defense — that’s the spine’s contract.
7. **Burp walkthrough.** Proxy a lab web application: observe in the proxy, manipulate one request by hand in the repeater, then automate one input with the intruder. Methodology before automation.
8. **Timeline your own attack.** Build a super-timeline covering steps 2 through 7 and note every artifact you left. If you can’t find your own footprints, you can’t find an attacker’s.

