This module is a discipline module: the methodology matters more than any single exploit. Run this exact loop on every lab machine, and document from minute one — screenshots and commands, because the report starts at recon.
1. **Enumerate like it’s 70% of the work — because it is.** Scan every port, identify every service, pin every version. The unexamined UDP service is where the vulnerability lives; check it before you touch the web app.
2. **Run the privesc checklists.** On a Linux shell: SUID binaries, cron jobs, writable paths, kernel version, sudo rights. On a Windows shell: unquoted paths, service permissions, AlwaysInstallElevated, tokens. Checklists beat inspiration.
3. **Walk the buffer-overflow rite of passage.** In the lab: fuzz to crash, find the offset, control EIP, build the exploit. You’re here to learn the stack — registers, memory layout, DEP/ASLR — disguised as an exploit.
4. **Chain a web vulnerability.** Take SQL injection to code execution in the lab (xp_cmdshell or a written shell), then pivot an SSRF to the metadata service at 169.254.169.254 — and then configure the IMDSv2 defense and confirm the chain breaks.
5. **Map Active Directory as a graph.** Ingest lab AD data into BloodHound-style thinking: find the shortest path to Domain Admin before you exploit anything. Then walk it: Kerberoasting or AS-REP roasting for the first credential, lateral movement for the next hop.
6. **Pair every attack with its detection.** Kerberoasting → event 4769 anomalies. DCSync → 4662. Golden Ticket → ticket metadata. Red teaches blue — log the detection alongside the exploit in your notes.
7. **Climb one CTF tier.** Solve at your current tier (T0 guided through T3 research) and write the solve up as a finding: title, evidence, impact, remediation — even for a game box. The report is the product.
8. **Exam logistics rehearsal.** If OSCP is on your roadmap: plan a 24-hour practice block — sleep strategy, meal breaks, and starting the report early. The report has its own 48-hour clock; logistics are part of the methodology.

