- **forward-deployed engineer** — the operator embedded with the customer: their network, their compliance, their timeline; diagnose fast, build to constraints, transfer everything.
- **transfer** — the FDE’s real deliverable: documentation and handoff so the customer operates the system after you leave.
- **unfamiliar estate** — a customer environment you’ve never seen; rapid diagnosis there is the FDE’s core skill.
- **AI triage summarization** — models drafting incident summaries from alert context; the analyst verifies before acting.
- **KQL from English** — AI-generated hunt queries; useful drafts that get a human check before they touch production data.
- **verification discipline** — the model drafts, the analyst decides; every AI output gets a human check before action.
- **AI-assisted hunting** — hypothesis generation from threat intel and anomaly narration; the amplifier, not the analyst.
- **practitioner-led learning** — war stories over lectures; operators carry the tacit knowledge slides can’t.
- **threat modeling** — enumerating what you’re defending, from whom, and where it breaks — early, at design time.
- **STRIDE** — Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege; applied per component.
- **DREAD** — Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability; the ranking scale for what STRIDE finds.
- **mitigations with owners** — the only acceptable threat-model output: each finding paired with a fix and a name.
- **model rot** — threat models decay on change like milk, not wine; a new data flow triggers a new session.
- **secure defaults** — deny-by-default, least privilege out of the box, APIs that are hard to misuse; defaults decide outcomes.
- **deny-by-default** — nothing is allowed until explicitly permitted; the default posture that defeats most breaches.
- **least privilege** — every component gets the minimum access it needs; the principle behind the default.
- **security review as a service** — findings delivered with fixes and SLAs by severity; the review that unblocks instead of blocking.
- **review SLA** — critical findings in hours, highs in days; predictability is what turns security from gate to partner.
- **shipping at the boundary** — the customer’s auditors, change windows, and rollback needs; security that ships beats perfect security that doesn’t.
- **auditor-ready documentation** — writing like an auditor reads it, because at the boundary one will.

