<text x="360.0" y="130" font-family="ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,SF Mono,Menlo,Consolas,monospace" font-size="11" fill="#8b949e" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="400" opacity="1.0" class="a-node" style="animation-delay:1.75s">diagnose fast, build to constraints</text></svg><figcaption style="margin-top:.6em;color:#8b949e;font-size:.85em;text-align:center"><strong>Fig. IL06-FDE-D1 — </strong>The forward-deployed engineer lives inside the customer&#x27;s constraints — and the real deliverable is transfer: documentation and handoff.</figcaption></figure>

Security engineering at the boundary is a practice discipline: model, decide, ship, hand off. Run this sequence against a sample system — a small web service with a database and an admin panel is plenty.
1. **Scope the model.** List what you’re defending, from whom, and the trust boundaries. One whiteboard, one hour — a whiteboard hour now saves a sprint of rework later.
2. **STRIDE per component.** Walk each component through Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, DoS, and Elevation. Write each finding as a concrete abuse case, not a vague worry.
3. **Rank with DREAD.** Score Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, and Discoverability for each finding. The ranking is what turns a list into a plan.
4. **Assign mitigations with owners.** Every finding gets a fix and a name. A model without mitigations is a diagram — say that out loud before you close the session.
5. **Harden the defaults.** Apply deny-by-default and least privilege to the sample service: remove the permissive default config, scope the service account, choose the safe API. Defaults decide outcomes — most breaches exploit the default, not the exotic.
6. **Run the review as a service.** Write up your findings the way a review should land: each with its fix, each with an SLA (critical in hours, highs in days). Predictability builds trust.
7. **AI-amplify one step.** Have an AI tool draft the incident summary or a KQL hunt for your sample system — then verify every line before you’d act on it. The model drafts, you decide.
8. **Write the handoff.** Produce the transfer document: architecture, credentials handling, runbooks, rollback plan. The customer operates it after you leave — that’s the deliverable.

